"\n\n<h3>I. Two Fronts of the Same Remainder</h3>\n\n<p>The McKinley years present the remainder on two fronts simultaneously — and the two fronts, traced to their root, are the same thing wearing different faces.</p>\n\n<p>The domestic front: the People's Party and the election of 1896 brought to its national climax the claim of farmers, debtors, and workers that the industrial economic order was squeezing them out of the promise of equality. The foreign front: the war of 1898 and its settlements turned what began as an external conflict into an internal constitutional problem. A polity that founded itself on equality and the consent of the governed now had to explain how it could legitimately govern overseas territories whose populations had neither been asked for their consent nor offered equality.</p>\n\n<p>In one sentence: 1896 shows how the construct absorbed or defeated internal social demands; 1898–1901 shows how it internalized an external empire as a constitutional question it could not resolve. This was not an abstract retrospective observation. The anti-imperialists of the time said it explicitly, in the language of the Declaration of Independence: the principles of this construct do not permit ruling people without their consent, and those principles cannot be applied differently by race.</p>\n\n<p>What makes these years uniquely revealing is the simultaneity. The remainder had previously surfaced in sequence — the race axis in one era, the class axis beginning to rise in the next. Here both erupted at nearly the same moment, from opposite directions. One set of people demanded more inclusion; another refused to be included at all. Both demands addressed the same underlying question: does this construct's universalism mean what it says?</p>\n\n<h3>II. Populism and the Election of 1896</h3>\n\n<p>The People's Party deserves to be understood through its actual platform, not through the lens of later anti-establishment politics. The Omaha Platform of 1892 made specific institutional demands: a national currency issued by the federal government rather than national banks; free and unlimited coinage of silver and gold at a ratio of 16:1; a graduated income tax; government ownership or control of railroads; and, in an appended resolution, direct election of U.S. senators. These were structural proposals, not mere grievance.</p>\n\n<p>The election of 1896 brought these demands to the highest point they would reach in national politics. William Jennings Bryan, nominated by the Democrats in Chicago after his \"Cross of Gold\" speech, fused the People's Party's agrarian coalition with the Democratic Party's Southern base and Western states. His central image — that labor could not be crucified on a cross of gold — moralized the currency question, transforming a technical financial arrangement into a symbol of economic subordination.</p>\n\n<p>McKinley won with 271 electoral votes and 51.1 percent of the popular vote. The Republican campaign, managed by Mark Hanna, raised approximately four million dollars — mostly from manufacturers who wanted tariff protection and bankers who wanted the gold standard — and deployed it in industrial-scale organizing and canvassing. But reducing 1896 to a bought election misses the structural reality: McKinley genuinely assembled a broader coalition, winning Northern cities, industrial workers, and commercial interests who believed currency stability meant jobs and that free silver meant inflation.</p>\n\n<p>The People's Party collapsed after 1896, but its agenda did not disappear. Direct election of senators was enacted by the Seventeenth Amendment in 1913. A graduated income tax became law that same year through the Sixteenth Amendment. Federal railroad regulation was substantially strengthened in the Roosevelt years. The platform's specific demands became, piecemeal, the Progressive Era's accomplishments. The remainder was defeated in 1896 — not extinguished.</p>\n\n<h3>III. From the Cuba Crisis to War with Spain</h3>\n\n<p>The crisis that became the Spanish-American War developed through Cuban insurgency, Spanish counterinsurgency, American media coverage, and finally a set of decisions in Washington. Cuba's independence revolt resumed in 1895; Spain's General Weyler responded with a reconcentration policy that herded civilians into camps, producing widespread suffering and death. The Hearst and Pulitzer papers covered these events with a combination of genuine atrocity reporting and sensationalized fabrication. Yellow journalism shaped the public climate but did not create the underlying policy conflict between American interests and Spanish colonial rule.</p>\n\n<p>McKinley initially sought to avoid war. He sent the battleship Maine to Havana harbor in January 1898 to protect American citizens and cool jingoistic sentiment. On February 15, the Maine exploded in the harbor with the loss of 266 men. American newspapers and hawks immediately named Spain. Modern forensic examination, particularly a 1976 Naval Institute analysis led by Admiral Rickover, found no technical evidence of an external explosion and concluded an internal accident — probably a coal bunker fire igniting adjacent ammunition — was more consistent with the evidence. A 1998 computer modeling study argued again for external explosion. The accurate statement is that modern research does not support the original Spanish-sabotage narrative, though the precise cause remains contested.</p>\n\n<p>McKinley's April 11 war message to Congress framed intervention as humanitarian — \"the forcible intervention of the United States as a neutral to stop the war.\" The gap between this public framing and what the war actually produced is the essay's structural hinge. The war was packaged as humanitarian rescue; its result was an overseas territorial empire.</p>\n\n<h3>IV. A Short War and the Birth of an Overseas Empire</h3>\n\n<p>Secretary of State John Hay called it \"a splendid little war.\" Dewey's Asiatic Squadron destroyed the Spanish fleet at Manila Bay on May 1, 1898; U.S. forces landed in Cuba and isolated the Spanish garrison; Spain sued for peace in August. The entire conflict lasted four months.</p>\n\n<p>The Treaty of Paris settled the results. Spain relinquished sovereignty over Cuba, ceded Puerto Rico, Guam, and the Philippines to the United States, and received twenty million dollars. A joint congressional resolution had already annexed Hawaii earlier that year. Together, Cuba (under American military occupation and then under the Platt Amendment's protectorate clauses), Puerto Rico, Guam, the Philippines, and Hawaii represented a rapid conversion from continental republic to Pacific-and-Caribbean empire.</p>\n\n<p>The empire also shifted the internal balance of constitutional power. Gilded Age presidents had been relatively weak against Congress. An overseas empire naturally concentrated authority at the executive end: the commander-in-chief conducts wars, the administration governs territories, treaty negotiations belong to the president. McKinley's annexation of Hawaii by joint resolution — sidestepping a treaty requiring two-thirds Senate approval — and his management of the Paris peace terms were acts that Congress could influence at the margins but not direct. Empire and executive revival grew together.</p>\n\n<p>Cuba illustrates the gap between professed purpose and actual control with documentary precision. The Teller Amendment of April 1898 declared that the United States \"hereby disclaims any disposition or intention to exercise sovereignty, jurisdiction, or control over said island.\" The Platt Amendment of 1901 required Cuba, as a condition of independence, to accept American rights of intervention \"for the preservation of Cuban independence\" and \"the maintenance of a government adequate for protecting life, property, and individual liberty,\" and to lease naval bases. Cuba's constitutional convention incorporated the Platt Amendment under pressure. The distance from Teller to Platt is the distance between universalist principle and imperial practice — measured, in this case, in four years and in treaty text.</p>\n\n<h3>V. The Philippine War</h3>\n\n<p>The Philippine-American War demands honest treatment. Filipino nationalists under Emilio Aguinaldo had allied with American forces against Spain in the expectation that the United States would support independence. When the Treaty of Paris transferred the Philippines to American sovereignty, they established the Philippine Republic and fought for it. The American government called this an insurrection — a term that preemptively classified Filipinos as rebels against American authority rather than as adversaries in a war of independence.</p>\n\n<p>The war proceeded in two phases: conventional fighting from February to November 1899, followed by guerrilla resistance until Aguinaldo's capture in 1901 and sporadic fighting until 1902. American conduct included acts that both contemporaries and subsequent official acknowledgment have identified as atrocities: the waterboarding of prisoners (referred to at the time as the \"water cure\"), the burning of villages, reconcentration camps for civilians, and reprisals against civilian populations. These were not marginal incidents but documented aspects of the counterinsurgency campaign.</p>\n\n<p>Casualty estimates vary considerably, but the ranges are significant. American deaths exceeded 4,200. Filipino combatant deaths exceeded 20,000. Filipino civilian deaths from violence, famine, and disease are estimated in the mainstream historiography at 200,000 to perhaps 320,000; higher figures circulate in Philippine nationalist historiography and some American scholarship. Whatever the precise count, the scale of suffering was enormous.</p>\n\n<p>The structural parallel with the domestic history is uncomfortable but unmistakable. When the racial remainder rose against Jim Crow — when Black Southerners voted, organized, or resisted — the construct's answer was violent suppression: the Klan, the Redeemer governments, the withdrawal of federal protection. When the Philippine remainder rose against American colonial rule, the construct's answer was also violent suppression: troops, waterboarding, burning villages. The racial hierarchy invoked in the Insular Cases' language about \"alien races\" and the inapplicability of \"Anglo-Saxon principles\" echoed the language used to justify Jim Crow. The logic was the same chessboard.</p>\n\n<h3>VI. The Anti-Imperialist Counter-Attack</h3>\n\n<p>American opposition to the empire was immediate and distinguished. The Anti-Imperialist League drew Mark Twain, Andrew Carnegie, William James, Samuel Gompers, and former president Grover Cleveland, among others. Their 1899 platform stated the point directly: the consent of the governed is the only legitimate basis for government, and this principle cannot be differentiated by race or color.</p>\n\n<p>What gives this opposition its structural significance in this series is not its political effectiveness — it ultimately failed to prevent annexation or the war — but its method of argument. The anti-imperialists were not appealing to a foreign ideology. They were using the construct's own founding texts against its own conduct. James wrote that America had \"vomited\" the Declaration of Independence and Washington's Farewell Address. This is exactly the move that had been made before: abolitionism used the Declaration against slavery; Harlan's dissent used it against Plessy; the anti-imperialists used it against Philippine conquest. The founding promise, once written, becomes a permanent standard available to anyone who wants to measure the gap between the construct's principles and its practice.</p>\n\n<p>This is the deepest structural reason the remainder cannot be closed. The construct wrote a promise into its founding documents that it has never been able to fully keep and has never been willing to formally revoke. It declared all men equal and governed slaves; it promised self-government and ruled colonies; it said consent was the source of legitimate authority and fought wars against populations that withheld that consent. The only way to eliminate the gap would be either to actually fulfill the promise or to officially withdraw it. The construct could do neither. So the promise remains — written, visible, a permanent measuring stick that every generation of the excluded or their advocates can pick up and use.</p>\n\n<h3>VII. Does the Constitution Follow the Flag? The Insular Cases</h3>\n\n<p>The legal question was whether the Constitution applied automatically to newly acquired territories. If it did, colonial governance would be constitutionally constrained in ways that might make the empire difficult to maintain. If it did not, the United States would have to acknowledge that it could acquire and rule territory without extending constitutional protections — that it was, in constitutional terms, a different kind of polity than it had claimed to be.</p>\n\n<p>The Insular Cases of 1901 — particularly Downes v. Bidwell — produced a deeply fragmented result. Justice Brown's plurality opinion held that Puerto Rico was \"a territory appended to and belonging to the United States, but not a part of the United States\" for purposes of the revenue uniformity clause. The same opinion contained explicit references to \"alien races\" differing \"from us in religion, customs, laws, methods of taxation, and modes of thought\" and to the difficulty of extending \"Anglo-Saxon principles\" to such populations. Justice White's concurring opinion — which eventually became the controlling doctrine — distinguished between \"incorporated\" territories (destined for statehood, full constitutional protection) and \"unincorporated\" territories (not so destined, limited constitutional protection).</p>\n\n<p>What the Insular Cases accomplished was a third option for handling the remainder, alongside inclusion and exclusion. The Court invented a legal device that suspended the question indefinitely. \"Belonging to but not a part of\" the United States was a logically contradictory formulation — but it was functionally useful. It allowed the construct to claim sovereignty and collect revenue from Puerto Rico and the Philippines while deferring or denying their populations' claims to equal constitutional status. The remainder was not included, not excluded, but held in a deliberately ambiguous intermediate zone, belonging and not belonging simultaneously.</p>\n\n<p>This third option — indefinite suspension — is in some ways the most revealing of the construct's responses to the remainder. Pressing the remainder toward the center acknowledges it; pushing it back to the margin suppresses it. Suspension does neither. It packages an unresolved contradiction in legal language designed to make it appear resolved. \"Belonging to but not part of\" is a phrase that sounds like a classification but describes a permanent evasion. The contradiction it contains has not been resolved. More than a century later, the legal status of Puerto Rico is still governed by Insular Cases doctrine.</p>\n\n<h3>VIII. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>The McKinley years delivered the remainder on two simultaneous fronts. On the domestic front, the People's Party brought economic grievance — the squeeze of railroad pricing, credit contraction, and agricultural debt — to its highest point of political expression. The construct's response was double: electoral defeat of free silver, followed by progressive-era absorption of substantial portions of the Populist agenda. The remainder was not destroyed in 1896; it was left as an unfinished list for the next generation.</p>\n\n<p>On the foreign front, 1898 converted an external conflict into an internal constitutional problem that the construct proved unable to resolve. A polity founded on consent and equality had acquired sovereignty over millions of people who had neither consented nor been offered equality. The answer — the Insular Cases and the theory of unincorporated territories — was not a resolution but a legal device for suspending the question. The remainder was neither included nor excluded but placed in a permanently ambiguous middle zone.</p>\n\n<p>The anti-imperialists' argument illuminates the series' central structural point. The construct's founding texts contain a universalist promise that its practice has never fulfilled and its institutions have never revoked. Every generation of the excluded, and every generation that speaks for them, can pick up the Declaration's language and use it to measure how far the practice falls short of the principle. That measuring stick is the deepest reason the remainder cannot be closed. It is inscribed in the construct's own birth certificate, available for use by anyone willing to read it literally.</p>\n\n      "
